Some experimental context:

- Our experiment aims to test and validate the use of our own visual stimulator to assess color vision deficiencies. We have started testing congenital color vision deficiencies (CVD), and we aim to assess other groups that have acquired color vision deficiencies (maybe PD).

- The experiment has several kinds of data:
    - Standardized scores (FM100).
    - Manual (behavioral test) with our stimulator
    - EEG-based grid stimulator.

- For some participants, we have repeated the experiment with the goal of assessing the test retest reliability of our data. A big part of things is whethere these data are repeatable, how good can we work with this for it to become a clinical test.

- Details about behavioral (manual) test Behavior-based CV assessment – The visual stimulator was used to present a single stimulus that alternated at 10 Hz between the yellow LED (i.e., monochromatic light source) and a combination of light from the red and green LEDs (i.e., the dichromatic light source). The luminance of the yellow LED was fixed (2400 D/A units; ∼450 lux). During the experiments, the participant used the visual simulator’s input devices to independently control the luminances of the red (0–3200 D/A units; 0 to ∼675 lux) and green (0– 2000 D/A units; 0 to ∼675 lux) LEDs. Participants were positioned in front of the visual stimulator. A chin rest was used to help participants maintain a consistent distance (∼30 cm), vertical position, and horizontal position. Each participant’s goal during the behaviorbased CV assessment was to adjust the luminances of the red and green LEDs until they produced light that was the same color as the yellow LED. Perceptually, if the color of the alternating light sources (i.e., yellow LED vs red + green LEDs) was different, the visual stimulus appeared to flicker. After matching the color of the two light sources, participants pressed a button to save the final red and green LED luminance values. Participants repeated this process 20 times (i.e., we denote each of these repetitions as a trial). The luminance settings of the red and green LEDs were randomized at the beginning of each trial. A random offset was added to the A/D values of the rotary variable resistors (i.e., input devices) during each trial to prevent participants from simply turning the input devices to the same location.

- Details about EEG (grid) test -- EEG was recorded from participants. Participants completed four (or 3 ) runs of data collection. Each run consisted of a sequence of 100 trials and lasted ∼6.25 minutes. During each trial, participants were asked to attend to a stimulus generated by the visual stimulator for three  seconds. Stimulus onsets and offsets were captured using a digital trigger. There was a 0.75-second inter-stimulus interval (ISI) between trials. The visual stimulus alternated at 10 Hz between the yellow LED and a combination of light from the red and green LEDs; it was the same visual stimulus used in the behavior-based CV assessment task. The luminance of the yellow LED was the same for all 100 trials. The luminances of the red and green LEDs were different during each of the 100 trials. Ten linearly spaced values were selected for each LED (0–3200 D/A units for red; 0–2000 D/A units for green) forming a 10 × 10 search grid. The different settings were presented in the same order for each run and participant; they were ordered to minimize abrupt perceptual changes between trials and slowly increase in overall brightness.

- As you can tell the behavioral and EEG test are very similar, in fact, we use both under the premise of finding the metamer. signified by the click in the behavioral test, and the minimal ssvep size in the EEG test. I have been able to test this relationship berfore and we will go into that. However, the more pressing issue for me is to find whether there exists a relation between the standardized score (FM100) and behavioral data, or FM100 and EEG data. I am interested on understanding if even for healthy participants (HC), we can see some trends of lower color vision that is aparent in the tests. 
